@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# Writing the brief
+
+Gemini receives only the brief plus its workspace/configured context, not your
+conversation. Write one bounded task per brief:
+
+```text
+<task>
+Goal: ...
+Current state and relevant paths: ...
+Allowed changes: ...
+Leave untouched: ...
+</task>
+<verification_loop>
+Run these exact project gates: ...
+Fix in-scope failures and report any blocked gates.
+</verification_loop>
+<action_safety>
+Do not commit, push, publish, install dependencies, or touch unrelated files.
+Stop for approval before irreversible operations or accessing sensitive data.
+</action_safety>
+<structured_output_contract>
+Report implementation, files changed, exact gates/outcomes, and unresolved limits.
+</structured_output_contract>
+```
+
+Use real paths and commands grounded in the repository. Keep secrets out of the
+brief and artifacts. The relay stores `brief.txt` and streams it on stdin with a
+fixed `--prompt`; the user text never reaches the Windows shell argv. Gemini may
+interpret its own prompt syntax (including file references), so review the brief.
+
+List path scope as instructions, never as a sandbox boundary. Start from a clean
+tree; otherwise record pre-existing changes before dispatch. Request an end report
+but rely on the diff and your own gate runs, not the implementer's claims.
+
+On resume, send a **delta brief**: what failed review, exact corrections, retained
+constraints, and the gates to rerun. Use the same `--cd`; Gemini sessions are
+project-scoped. Re-pass approval and model choices deliberately on each dispatch.
